"""Room programming policy: infer inhabited uses without overriding user intent."""
from .domain import canonical
from .house_planning import RECIPES
from .house_floor_details import floor_detail

RENDER_REFERENCE_POLICY = """Default artistic target: Assets/AtmosphericHouse/Scenes/House_clean_day.unity, an inhabited game interior. Unless the user requests night or a different mood, design for lateral daylight through windows, readable soft indirect shadows, restrained practical lamps, coherent clean material families and visible contact with the floor. Lighting settings or brighter exposure alone do not prove this look. Baked indirect lighting must be verified by a native bake receipt, never inferred from screenshots.
Composition target: purposeful primary furniture, stocked storage, coherent small prop clusters on supported surfaces, wall decoration, curtains, floor textiles and restrained lived-in details, while keeping a clear walkable route. Use catalogue prefabs at native scale. Unsupported wall mounting, material variants, curtains, or other requested details stay explicit unresolved needs, never substitute random unrelated props. Do not clone the reference room's furniture layout into every room, and do not add child-bedroom decoration to unrelated purposes. For a child bedroom use age-appropriate bed/storage, books and toys where available, soft textiles, personal wall details and storage baskets; preserve calm areas and play space.
"""


ROOM_POLICY = '''You control the layout, not a fixed furniture recipe. Coordinates use Unity Y-up, with X/Z as the floor plane; yaw 0 keeps native axes and positive yaw 90 turns native +Z toward world +X. functional_forward is the reviewed local use-facing direction, not necessarily +Z. Orient usable fronts toward accessible space, and place a desk chair on the use-facing side. Unknown fronts need visual review. For every group supply items with catalogue asset IDs, unique slot IDs, local offset_m [x,z], yaw_deg and support_slot (null for floor furniture). Supply anchor_m [world x,world z] and group yaw_deg. Coordinate contract: floor items (support_slot null) use offsets relative to the GROUP anchor. Supported items use offset_m and yaw_deg relative to the PARENT PREFAB LOCAL ORIGIN, not the group anchor or world position. Do not copy the nightstand group offset onto its lamp: use the centre of the selected qualified parent support rectangle in parent-local X/Z coordinates. The solver applies the parent position and rotation. The recipe label only describes purpose; it does NOT select the furniture. Pick furniture for the brief and scale, vary layouts by design intent, consider island desks, wall desks and different facing directions. Room origin is given below. Positions will be checked, not silently replaced with a template. Keep supports before children semantically; cycles are invalid. Unknown-front assets can be explicitly chosen but require visual orientation review; only qualified support_regions can carry props. Respect visually reviewed placement_modes: floor furniture uses null support_slot; horizontal_support requires an actual qualified parent. Wall/window-only items cannot be disguised as floor groups or tabletop props. Keep those needs unresolved until a supported mounting control is available. Unknown modes require further evidence. No arbitrary random variation or unsupported claims of qualification.
The native architecture builder supplies walls, floor, ceiling tiles and visible Lamp_ceiling_round ceiling lights with HDRP sources for every room. Ceiling_3x3 is a ceiling TILE, not a lamp. Do not invent an unsupported custom furnishing requirement for these built-in architectural elements. A specifically requested desk lamp or other fixture remains a separate need.
Design an inhabited room, not just the single furniture item named by its purpose.
First fulfil explicit requests and exclusions. Furnished is the default unless the user asks for empty,
minimal, shell-only, or an exact inventory. Infer useful complementary activities from the room purpose:
- office: work equipment, library/storage with books, greenery, and a reading or visitor corner when space allows;
- living room: seating, reading/storage, plants, and useful personal details;
- bedroom: sleeping, bedside storage, clothes storage, optional reading and greenery;
- kitchen/dining: preparation/eating first, storage and restrained useful details.
Choose a coherent subset for the actual dimensions and style, not every possible item. A bed in an office
is a mixed-use/guest-room decision: only include it when the brief suggests sleeping or guests. Do not
invent a bedroom in every office. Avoid adding a television just to create a reading corner.
The global user brief is the source of explicit requests; the generated room brief is not a user quote. Set each item priority explicitly when accessories differ from the functional group: primary furniture and user-requested items are mandatory; inferred accessories such as an extra pillow are preferred or optional. Omitted item priority inherits the group priority for legacy plans. Do not downgrade an existing mandatory item merely to hide a placement failure. The core room function and explicitly requested groups are mandatory; inferred complements are preferred or optional even if they appear in the generated room description. Place the
primary function first, then storage and complementary uses, then decoration. Preserve circulation;
never resize prefabs or enlarge an explicitly dimensioned room just to fit extra decoration.
For an inhabited game office, explicitly plan a floor layer: a rug near seating and, if the brief allows lived-in clutter, one or more coherent paper clusters near work/storage. Use available floor_assets in detail_assets; these are whole floor prefabs placed separately from tabletop props. Prefer smaller clusters that fit the room; do not carpet every gap. Clean/professional means restrained clutter, not no personal objects; horror/abandoned can use more. Respect explicit empty/minimal/clean-floor exclusions.
Select plants as freestanding plant groups, libraries as library groups, seating corners as reading
or lounge groups. Small books and stationery belong in detail_assets on qualified supports. For each supported detail, use an object with asset, support_group, support_slot and surface_id (null to choose any qualified patch on that exact support). support_group and support_slot identify your group id and item slot, including existing groups during enrichment. A missing, blocked or unqualified target remains unresolved; the solver will not move the item to unrelated furniture. Select a measured surface_id to distribute books across particular qualified shelf compartments. Use plain asset-name strings for floor details; historical string plans remain supported. Put stationery on the work desk and bedside lamps on bedside furniture by explicitly targeting those slots. Choose upright book rows, tabletop stacks, reading material and drawing supplies from the visually reviewed catalogue according to the intended activity, appearance and measured fit. There are no preferred prefab names. Use the catalogue review rationale to distinguish child-oriented choices from unsuitable alternatives; a qualified support is not a reason to replace a suitable asset with a familiar one. Include books with a library rather than leaving it bare. Do not
put large floor furniture in detail_assets. Use only catalogue names. For tabletop detail_assets use qualified assets. For explicit group items, choose by style and actual dimensions; an unknown front requires visual review rather than silently substituting the same qualified furniture. Do not repeat groups without
an activity/occupancy reason. Unavailable wall art, rugs or lighting must remain explicit needs when
requested; do not replace them with unrelated props. Leave calm areas rather than filling every gap.'''


def room_prompt(room,brief,assets):
    families={}
    for name,a in assets.items():
        path=a.get('prefab_path','');family=path.split('/Prefabs/')[-1].rsplit('/',1)[0] if '/Prefabs/' in path else 'other'
        families.setdefault(family,[]).append(name)
    catalogue=dict(room=room,brief=brief,resource_families=families,
        room_geometry=room.get('geometry'),asset_catalogue=[dict(id=n,size_m=a['bounds_size'],front_status=a['front_status'],placement_modes=a.get('placement_modes',[]),functional_forward=a.get('forward'),bounds_center_m=a['bounds_center'],prefab_path=a.get('prefab_path'),style_tags=a.get('style_tags',[]),support_regions=[dict(surface_id=r['surface_id'],rectangle_xz=r['rectangle_xz'],roles=r.get('roles',[])) for r in a.get('support_regions',[])]) for n,a in assets.items() if not any(k in a.get('prefab_path','') for k in ('/Building/','/Doors_drawers/'))],floor_assets=[dict(name=n,size_m=a['bounds_size']) for n,a in assets.items() if floor_detail(n,a)],
        qualified_assets=[n for n,a in assets.items() if a.get('front_status') in {'qualified','not_applicable'}],
        unqualified_assets=[n for n,a in assets.items() if a.get('front_status') not in {'qualified','not_applicable'} and not floor_detail(n,a)])
    return RENDER_REFERENCE_POLICY+ROOM_POLICY+'\nGroup purpose labels: '+canonical(list(RECIPES))+'\nYou choose every item and its placement; purpose labels never inject assets. Native placement checks decide what fits. Room and brief: '+canonical(catalogue)
